#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a74912-9939-5160-b2f2-fc9f75995b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478590} {:answer/id #uuid "489fb4e2-401a-51bd-8367-54c21281a25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478661} {:answer/id #uuid "af934f8e-6b47-50f3-976d-130962164ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478844} {:answer/id #uuid "d704c90e-1c68-5f35-9050-f1ea9764f3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478900} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d81125-ee3b-5e23-ba6b-3f5a3fc654e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af934f8e-6b47-50f3-976d-130962164ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478844} :answer-field/id #uuid "77f83ac4-379e-5a13-8c4c-6ea313e7d773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4437c604-b7f0-5633-b7e5-9971f03bd2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-165283" :question/problem "Given that $v(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=\\frac{∂v}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂r}=2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =(2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0466a8b0-0e72-50a8-9e40-0c1214a8a281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478566} {:answer/id #uuid "074a0854-45c8-5e8e-88a6-4963ee2622c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478571} {:answer/id #uuid "1a085611-6858-54c9-855d-19621bc6a77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478598} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1073de-8640-5caf-b844-a33c80d8ae74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478837} {:answer/id #uuid "d4935e49-5ceb-5de5-a639-1d44e14dc969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0466a8b0-0e72-50a8-9e40-0c1214a8a281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478566} :answer-field/id #uuid "52931c2c-52e1-5448-b25c-5e4f502aa192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7396bdb2-9333-5e99-8459-ac9d52a00034" :question/math-academy-id "q-165285" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79830e35-fb58-5c0c-8ffe-3455034ae32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368497} {:answer/id #uuid "e18a672b-de77-5df8-8f1b-84076247b048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368498} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7be39b-6734-5fbb-b31d-4ee6cfc206ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368499} {:answer/id #uuid "633b9946-97ef-5a0d-a217-10b115c3dbb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368500} {:answer/id #uuid "1cddd4cb-d4e0-5346-8409-af4fe2ea10fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cddd4cb-d4e0-5346-8409-af4fe2ea10fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368501} :answer-field/id #uuid "77fe4fb6-8827-5540-a442-722c8b1aa5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0722c4ed-9b45-5a82-9aca-63ef2538a12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37192" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6f0a484-c1df-59cd-8a67-536b8a6b6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d87c61-ce4c-51b4-a48d-e8a1bd1e34a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122842} {:answer/id #uuid "0f53b13e-3023-5aa6-80ef-b86a7b9a981e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478579} {:answer/id #uuid "19655a9a-cd78-5629-b63c-3d39429c6795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478596} {:answer/id #uuid "a74e8615-6e66-546e-bfc7-29b7cec5ca41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6f0a484-c1df-59cd-8a67-536b8a6b6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841} :answer-field/id #uuid "086dfdbb-92b2-5cae-9238-3f1a3c6feab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08b52ee2-9e81-5a5f-aeda-8fc812032d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-37196" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dcc952-87b3-5c50-8329-877474c9cc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358187} {:answer/id #uuid "7b26dce8-c86e-5f4c-be68-35bd9babb814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358821} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f856b1-f69a-596f-91ca-6e69e6d25660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359482} {:answer/id #uuid "d48510a2-a1cc-5ccc-a78d-0f55b5682108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359606} {:answer/id #uuid "ede18e52-59dd-5738-903a-565892bb8bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ede18e52-59dd-5738-903a-565892bb8bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359866} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f9d5fe-44a3-58bb-a9fc-01470465cbfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6edb4185-988d-5465-a73c-d87078eb5744" :question/math-academy-id "q-94770" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12433c4a-668f-57da-9582-b6ee6515dc42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508415} {:answer/id #uuid "5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508526} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a54554-40d8-5b1a-a681-1f39ccace6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508691} {:answer/id #uuid "d353fe5f-d6bc-5d38-8a44-d344cb894fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508730} {:answer/id #uuid "f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508526} :answer-field/id #uuid "53289756-546a-50f2-80e0-c0a1e0a48184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" :question/math-academy-id "q-95111" :question/problem "Given that $q(x,y)=xy,$ find $\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255b1439-4503-5ba1-9467-b4cbf23cf837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358086} {:answer/id #uuid "2826740b-006a-5fb8-af1b-b04cb4e430c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358112} {:answer/id #uuid "8f20dc0c-2f9c-59cb-9371-1e2bab94de58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358999} {:answer/id #uuid "c17a4d57-ab7f-5cad-9c18-15918d10a4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359449} {:answer/id #uuid "ff52e99a-a36a-570b-927e-6775bb16f2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff52e99a-a36a-570b-927e-6775bb16f2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997} :answer-field/id #uuid "d14ca8c8-8191-546e-952e-035f83d5d2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a5ea0e8-c2a8-56bd-bc0a-1183f811a786" :question/math-academy-id "q-95113" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)={y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe46cca-410f-5c55-88ef-8d80e7031ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358726} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7b642c-5011-5fe6-b8b3-8ba15e13f44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359374} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc682ef-5b2d-5c3c-8130-e2f1ce1ee474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} {:answer/id #uuid "e32325f6-ab86-53be-a9ca-0e5883831318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359761} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9162b2-a7fc-5e49-88a2-4b87f9188d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfc682ef-5b2d-5c3c-8130-e2f1ce1ee474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebf0a96-067c-5319-889e-a652fa3fcdea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09b6c584-862f-5f2c-8d59-a960b719bfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95116" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5558a820-fe68-5154-b605-abf1888ca002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358495} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccd388d-c735-55eb-b378-f7d1e760c7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358704} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7beea8-2165-59f1-b2bd-041b16b78155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358854} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb8186e-5270-5422-93d1-cdc25d726dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359269} {:answer/id #uuid "dbba7a33-6727-5aa3-8d25-973cb0049ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbba7a33-6727-5aa3-8d25-973cb0049ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359678} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0ec5fc-3755-5a5d-a8d1-a936b1188676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4ca4975-1827-563d-9fb6-1ff2551b2f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-95130" :question/problem "Given that $g(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r}=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }=-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a1ee6a-caac-58ee-afb5-38bc8bb1ed51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357819} {:answer/id #uuid "2228fe89-b437-5a7a-aa61-67383a3bc1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358049} {:answer/id #uuid "2486a9ac-6c70-5c04-9668-5c8f9093b469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358077} {:answer/id #uuid "c28adbb2-a46a-5fcd-bc0c-7c33f8291c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359459} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4